{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>Behold please, my lord.</b> My masters, it is profane language. And whenever you find the word סור followed by a mem, it means what it says. But if it is followed by an alef with patach under an open [syllable], its sense is that one should turn from his place and go to the caller, or to the place that is called, as in 'turn aside to me' (Judges 4:18)." "<b>And they said, No.</b> If you will not insist. And this word is an abundance of words, like the sense of placations, and likewise 'and iniquity and teraphim' [is] insistence (1 Samuel 15:23)."], :book :genesis, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 19, :notes {0 "Resolved 'רבותי' as 'my masters,' Ibn Ezra's standard address to his readers; the argument concerns the morphology of סורה ('turn aside') with mem versus alef suffixed."}, :source-witnesses [["On Your Way" "http://mobile.tora.ws/"] ["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:45:05.154386457Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}